    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1128">

                <pb facs="1146" n="860" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg860"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chargé in
                        Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-10-19T18:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 19, 1916, 6 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3497. Your 4452, 10th. Mr. Gerard states that he received no assurances from the
                    Berlin Foreign Office in regard to the exemption of laborers, other than those
                    made verbally that no more of the civil population of Lille, Roubaix, and
                    Tourcoing would be sent to labor in the fields.</p>
                <p>If you deem it advisable, you may informally and orally draw to the attention of
                    the Minister for Foreign Affairs the reported action of the military authorities
                    in Belgium and its alleged object, reminding him of the verbal assurances above
                    mentioned as being to some extent connected with the reported situation in
                    Belgium.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
